<speak>
<s>From <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="zʊhd">zuhd</phoneme> news, this is your briefing for <say-as interpret-as="date" format="dmy">23022026</say-as>.</s> <s>The sixth of <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="rɑmɑˈdˤɑːn">Ramadan</phoneme>, fourteen forty-seven.</s> <s>It's morning in <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="ˈmæk.kæ">Makkah</phoneme>, just after <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="fɑdʒr">Fajr</phoneme>.</s>
<break time="1s"/>
<s>The United States is withdrawing hundreds of troops from forward bases in Qatar and Bahrain — repositioning them out of Iranian missile range — as Oman confirmed that direct nuclear talks between Washington and Tehran are set for this Thursday in Geneva.</s> <s>Iran's foreign minister is offering full international monitoring of the nuclear program in exchange for sanctions relief, while Supreme Leader <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="xɑˈmɛniː">Khamenei</phoneme> has quietly ordered every senior official to designate a successor — a contingency Tehran now treats as a live possibility.</s> <s>Whether Thursday's talks hold or collapse may determine the Gulf's trajectory for a generation.</s>
<break time="900ms"/>
<s>In conflict and security.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>Mexico's army killed <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="neˈmesjo oseˈɣeɾa seɾˈβantes">Nemesio Oseguera Cervantes</phoneme> — known as El Mencho — the <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="hɑˈlɪskoʊ">Jalisco</phoneme> New Generation Cartel boss who carried a fifteen-million-dollar United States bounty.</s> <s>Within hours, the cartel's fighters launched coordinated reprisals across more than twenty states: highway blockades, vehicle burnings, airline cancellations from Puerto Vallarta, and gun battles that forced schools to close.</s> <s>Without their founder, the cartel's factions now face a succession struggle that may prove more violent than his removal.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Israeli forces have arrested more than a hundred Palestinians in the five days since Ramadan began — roughly twenty per day — while banning decorations at Al-Aqsa and blocking access to the mosque compound.</s> <s>The Palestinian Prisoner's Society reports severe beatings, home demolitions, and a starvation policy applied across nine thousand three hundred detainees, including three hundred and fifty children.</s> <s>Arrests are accelerating as the holy month continues.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Two days before the fourth anniversary of Russia's invasion, both sides escalated simultaneously.</s> <s>Eleven Ukrainian drones forced the closure of all four of Moscow's major airports — shutting one of the world's busiest air hubs for hours — while Russia answered overnight with two hundred and ninety-seven drones and fifty missiles targeting Ukraine's power plants and railways, cutting electricity to six regions in freezing temperatures.</s> <s>The anniversary arrives tomorrow, and no ceasefire is in sight.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>In Nigeria's northwest, armed men raided a village in Zamfara state, killing dozens and abducting women and children — the second mass attack in the region in a single week, after a separate assault killed fifty people days earlier.</s> <s>Years of such killings have drawn no lasting federal response, and no armed group has been held to account.</s> <s>The northwest remains one of West Africa's most persistent crises, with the least international attention.</s>
<break time="900ms"/>
<s>In politics.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>Hong Kong's Court of Appeal dismissed all twelve challenges brought by defendants in the city's largest national security trial, confirming that organizing an unofficial 2020 primary election constitutes subversion under Beijing's law.</s> <s>Forty-seven former lawmakers and activists must now serve sentences of up to ten years, with every level of Hong Kong's judiciary having upheld the convictions.</s> <s>The ruling closes the last legal avenue for a generation of the city's democratic opposition.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>In Venezuela, two hundred and fourteen political detainees began a hunger strike after the country's new amnesty law excluded the most common charge keeping them imprisoned: military-related offenses.</s> <s>The law was passed under United States pressure but written to omit the very detainees most threatening to the government — fifteen hundred amnesty applications remain unprocessed.</s> <s>Strikers are demanding the law be expanded to cover military charges, a test of how far Washington is willing to push.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Ghana's President <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="mɑˈhɑːmɑ">Mahama</phoneme> will bring a resolution to the United Nations General Assembly next month, backed by forty African Union members, formally seeking recognition of the transatlantic slave trade as the gravest crime against humanity.</s> <s>No colonial power has ever allowed this designation to be codified in international law.</s> <s>The vote in March will test whether the General Assembly can name what powerful states have spent centuries refusing to name.</s>
<break time="900ms"/>
<s>On the economy.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>Six justices struck down the Trump administration's emergency tariffs on Thursday — and within hours, Washington imposed a fifteen percent global surcharge under a separate legal authority that allows temporary tariffs for up to one hundred and fifty days.</s> <s>China's Commerce Ministry demanded that all tariffs be canceled, but the new surcharge requires no court approval and expires in August.</s> <s>The administration has given itself a five-month window to negotiate — or simply wait it out.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>No cattle appeared at the Paris Agriculture Fair for the first time in its history — lumpy skin disease forced government-ordered culls across France, the European Union's largest agricultural producer.</s> <s>Empty pens arrived alongside warnings that the bloc has suffered fifteen years of failed farm policy.</s> <s>French farmers who blockaded Paris last year are watching to see if empty stalls finally force a reckoning.</s>
<break time="900ms"/>
<s>In science.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>Nearly seven million bitcoin — worth roughly four hundred and forty billion dollars — sit in wallets whose cryptographic protections quantum computers will eventually break.</s> <s>Early transactions, including the estimated one million coins belonging to Bitcoin's anonymous creator, permanently exposed their public keys on-chain, and researchers now estimate that machines capable of cracking them are two to three decades away.</s> <s>The window to migrate those funds is open — but narrowing.</s>
<break time="900ms"/>
<s>In technology.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>The Marshall Islands has become the first nation to launch a universal basic income — eight hundred dollars a year delivered to all forty thousand citizens by bank transfer or cryptocurrency, reaching remote atolls that have no conventional banks.</s> <s>Funding comes from the country's military access compact with the United States, converting sovereignty into social policy.</s> <s>For a nation whose islands face permanent inundation from rising seas, the question is whether the payments outlast the land.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Six African nations now operate their space programs on infrastructure that China built and funded — from ground stations in Namibia to satellite assembly facilities in Egypt.</s> <s>Every upgrade and every repair requires returning to Beijing, replicating a model of dependency that mirrors older patterns of infrastructure investment on the continent.</s> <s>Africa's orbital ambitions are real and growing; the question is who holds the keys to the hardware.</s>
<break time="1s"/>
<s>That's your briefing from <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="zʊhd">zuhd</phoneme> news.</s>
<break time="500ms"/>
<s>We're back in twelve hours.</s>
<break time="300ms"/>
<s><phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="ʔɪn ʃæːʔ ɑɫ.ɫɑːh">إن شاء الله</phoneme>.</s>
</speak>